.SH NAME
ssoossh-nss-cache - Mark cached entries stale so their next lookup asks the server.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBssoossh-nss cache -E | -u NAME | -g NAME | -U | -G [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Marks entries stale through the running daemon, like sss_cache: a stale entry is refetched on its next lookup and is still served while the server is unreachable. Nothing is deleted. Remembered misses for the selection are forgotten too.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-E\fP, \fB--everything\fP[=false]
	every user and group

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--group\fP=""
	one group, by name

.PP
\fB-G\fP, \fB--groups\fP[=false]
	every group

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for cache

.PP
\fB-u\fP, \fB--user\fP=""
	one user, by name

.PP
\fB-U\fP, \fB--users\fP[=false]
	every user


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--admin-socket\fP="/run/ssoossh-nss/admin.sock"
	the admin socket the CLI and the daemon share

.PP
\fB-c\fP, \fB--config\fP="/etc/ssoossh/nss.yaml"
	the daemon's configuration file

.PP
\fB--overrides\fP="/etc/ssoossh/nss-overrides.yaml"
	the host override file


